WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.610
BRIT CRUISE: After World War II,
with most of Europe in ruins,

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:11.300
tension grew between the Soviet
Union and the United States.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.740
It was clear that the next
global superpower required

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:18.130
the ability to both launch
and successfully defend

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:20.620
nuclear attacks from
intercontinental ballistic

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:21.800
missiles.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.570
In North America, the most
vulnerable point of attack

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:26.040
was over the North Pole.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:29.640
So in 1958, a joint effort
between United States

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.850
and Canada was established known
as NORAD, or North American

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:35.790
Aerospace Defense Command.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:39.430
An important line of defense
was the semiautomatic ground

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:40.890
environment.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:44.900
It was an automated system of
over 100 long-distance radars

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.220
scattered across North America.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.640
They were connected to
computerized radar stations

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.810
that transmitted tracking data
using telephone lines or radio

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:55.820
waves.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:59.350
All of this radar information
was fed into a primary Warning

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:04.170
Center buried a mile deep inside
Cheyenne Mountain in Colorado.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:07.470
This application of machine
to machine communication

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:10.410
allowed operators to make
split-second decisions

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:14.520
using information transmitted
and processed automatically

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:15.910
by computers.

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:20.060
This idea of being online was
quickly adapted and advanced

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.200
by universities in
the following years

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.845
as they understood the potential
of computer networking.

00:01:24.845 --> 00:01:25.180
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:27.596
SPEAKER 1: The thing that makes
the computer communication

00:01:27.596 --> 00:01:31.680
network special is that it puts
the workers, that be the team

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.300
members who are geographically
distributed, in touch

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.320
not only with one another,
but with the information

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.610
base with which they
work all the time.

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:42.260
And this is obviously going to
make a tremendous difference

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:46.360
in how we plan, organize,
and execute almost everything

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.550
of any intellectual consequence.

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:52.590
If we get into a mode in
which everything is handled

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:55.150
electronically and your
only identification is

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:57.910
some little plastic thing
you stick into the machinery,

00:01:57.910 --> 00:01:59.330
then I can imagine
that they want

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:02.590
to get that settled up with your
bank account just right now,

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:04.630
and put it through
all the checks.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.230
And that would require network.

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:06.530
[END VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:07.905
BRIT CRUISE: Money
transfers were

00:02:07.905 --> 00:02:10.250
just one of a growing
number of applications which

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:13.140
required encryption
to remain secure.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.690
And as the internet grew
to encompass millions

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:18.960
around the world, a
new problem emerged.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.370
At the time, encryption
required two parties

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:25.520
to first share a secret
random number, known as a key.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.930
So how could two people
who have never met

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:32.095
agree on a secret shared
key without letting

00:02:32.095 --> 00:02:37.440
Eve, who is always listening,
also obtain a copy?

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.240
In 1976, Whitfield
Diffie and Martin Hellman

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:44.070
devised an amazing
trick to do this.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:48.610
First, let's explore how this
trick is done using colors.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:51.960
How could Alice and Bob
agree on a secret color

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.050
without Eve finding it out?

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:56.880
The trick is based on two facts.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:02.020
One, it's easy to mix two colors
together to make a third color.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:05.230
And two, given a
mixed color, it's

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:10.510
hard to reverse it in order to
find the exact original colors.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:12.900
This is the basis for a lock.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.350
Easy in one direction.

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:17.000
Hard in the reverse direction.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.480
This is known as a
one-way function.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.770
Now, the solution
works as follows.

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:27.360
First, they agree publicly
on a starting color.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.480
Say, yellow.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:35.250
Next, Alice and Bob both
randomly select private colors

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.310
and mix them into
the public yellow

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:40.730
in order to disguise
their private colors.

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:43.400
Now, Alice keeps
her private color

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.020
and sends her mixture to Bob.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:50.280
And Bob keeps his private color
and sends his mixture to Alice.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:57.180
Now the heart of the trick.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:00.300
Alice and Bob add
their private colors

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:03.160
to the other person's
mixture and arrived

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.180
at a shared secret color.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:13.310
Notice how Eve it is unable
to determine this exact color,

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:17.199
since she needs one of their
private colors to do so.

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:23.870
And that is the trick.

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:27.940
Now to do this with numbers,
we need a numerical procedure,

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:31.990
which is easy in one direction
and hard in the other.

